<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 6</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 6</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 6)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>05-27 Workflow Coaching</p><p>Plaud file id: dbbcfbec76a5563c6169f18ed40a32c7</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance Review and Workflow Coaching</p><p>&gt; **Overview:** This summary covers a one-to-one meeting between **Bob Duncan** (manager) and **Paul Banyard** (adviser) to review Paul&#x27;s monthly business performance against targets and to deliver structured, hands-on coaching on client workflow management. The session addresses Paul&#x27;s current mortgage and protection figures, works through a series of overdue and miscategorised tasks within their Platform and Midas systems, and concludes with a clear strategic directive to close the critical gap in protection sales before month-end.</p><p>---</p><p>## Business Performance and Target Analysis</p><p>The meeting opened with Bob prompting Paul to pull up his spreadsheet, specifically his **21-day report**, the **protection callbacks** being managed through MAB, and his **written and banked business targets** for the current financial year. Paul acknowledged he still needed to update both the 21-day report and the protection callbacks report, having encountered a loading issue with the MAB protection calls the previous week.</p><p>### Mortgage Figures — Breakdown and Reporting</p><p>Paul initially reported approximately **£3,000 in mortgages and £1,100 in protection** written for the month. Bob stressed that this was insufficient granularity and directed Paul to break the mortgage total down by **product type** — specifically separating **new purchase cases** from **renewal cases**. Bob recommended Paul use the **&quot;Added to System&quot; report within Midas Pro**, run by adviser and exported, to achieve this breakdown clearly.</p><p>Working through the numbers together, the following figures were confirmed for the month:</p><p>| Category | Detail |</p><p>| --- | --- |</p><p>| **Mortgage sub-totals** | £31,787 + £461.40 + £87.01 + £1,650.72 |</p><p>| **Total Actual Written** | **£279,226.80** |</p><p>| **Actual Banked** | **£4,140.81** |</p><p>| **Renewal Cases** | 4 |</p><p>| **New Purchase Cases** | 3 (including remortgage cases treated as new business) |</p><p>| **Protection / Insurance Apps** | 1 |</p><p>Bob clarified that whilst one case appeared as a remortgage in the system, it should be counted as a **purchase case** for reporting purposes, as it was not a standard renewal lead. Charlotte&#x27;s case was identified as a new purchase.</p><p>### Position Against Target</p><p>As of the **27th of the month**, Paul requires **two further purchase cases** to meet his monthly mortgage target, placing him slightly behind the curve. However, Paul confirmed he has **two potential cases in progress** — one already on documents — giving reasonable cause for optimism, subject to documentation being received in time.</p><p>On protection, the picture is more concerning. Paul currently has just **one insurance application** against a **quarterly target of eight**, representing a conversion rate of approximately **16% for the month**. Bob acknowledged that Paul is broadly on track for his mortgage numbers but was explicit that protection is the performance gap requiring urgent attention.</p><p>---</p><p>## Client Workflow and Process Coaching</p><p>With Paul noting that business &quot;felt quiet,&quot; Bob immediately challenged this framing, using it as an opportunity to audit the state of Paul&#x27;s workflows on **Platform** and **Midas**. Paul confirmed he had **five overdue tasks** — all falling late from the 26th. Bob guided Paul through each case in turn, providing step-by-step instruction on correct task resolution and workflow progression.</p><p>### Client: New Build Reservation (Robert Thurston / New Build Unit Client)</p><p>This case prompted the most detailed coaching. The client had **reserved a new build unit** and been referred to Paula to pay a deposit, with document requests already sent via Teams. Paul had become stuck because the workflow options did not appear to match the actual stage of the case — a consequence of having entered the workflow via the **&quot;Actively Looking&quot;** path rather than the correct **&quot;Offer Accepted / DIP&quot;** route.</p><p>Bob walked Paul through the correct sequence:</p><p> 1. Resolve the **&quot;Attended&quot;** task at the top of the workflow.</p><p> 2. Select **&quot;DIP Required&quot;** → Resolve → Refresh.</p><p> 3. Mark the **DIP as Passed** (Halifax approved at 85% LTV, appropriate for a home mover) → Resolve → Refresh.</p><p> 4. Confirm **documents are required** → Resolve.</p><p> 5. Confirm **comments have not yet been sent** (Paul confirmed this was outstanding).</p><p> 6. Confirm **EIDV/ID checks** have been completed and passed.</p><p> 7. Remove redundant tasks (email passport, DEP tasks already completed).</p><p> 8. Mark **documents as requested** → Resolve → Refresh — this automatically assigns a task to **Sean** to review documents and update Paul on any gaps.</p><p> 9. Complete the **pre-advice checklist** review (comment us, fact find, DIP, KFI, home report, illustrations, added to system status).</p><p>10. Progress to **&quot;Book Recommendation&quot;** stage — leaving Sean to manage the document review and appointment booking, given the client is a friend of Megan&#x27;s.</p><p>Bob made clear that the **document review and chasing task now sits with Sean**, not Paul. Once Sean confirms documents are in order, Paul will receive an automated notification and can proceed to book the advice appointment. If documents arrive before the end of the week, the case could realistically be booked in promptly.</p><p>The key coaching point was conceptual: **a new build reservation should be treated as equivalent to an &quot;offer accepted,&quot;** and Paul should have entered the workflow at that stage from the outset. Bob suggested that the workflow wording might be updated in future to read **&quot;Offer Accepted / Reserve New Build&quot;** to avoid similar confusion. More broadly, Bob advised Paul not to interpret workflow prompts too literally — the exact wording will never cover every scenario — but to **think logically about the client journey** and proceed accordingly. Crucially, rather than allowing tasks to fall overdue, Paul was urged to contact Bob directly for a quick steer whenever the workflow options appear ambiguous.</p><p>### Client: Ramesh (Plot of Land / Self-Build Referral)</p><p>Paul had been waiting on Bob to provide referral details for a specialist lender before updating this task. Bob confirmed the contact as **Harry at Buildstore** (a self-build specialist) and sent the details to Paul by email during the meeting.</p><p>Paul raised a clarification regarding fees: as the firm cannot directly handle the self-build case, he questioned whether any fee should be communicated to the client. Bob&#x27;s guidance was clear — whilst no fee would be charged for the referral itself, Paul should still **provide an indicative figure** (approximately **£1,000**) so the client understands what to expect once the mainstream application stage is reached, noting that the specialist firm will confirm their own charges.</p><p>The workflow for Ramesh was resolved as follows: task marked as **&quot;Attended / Next Steps&quot;** → client status set to **&quot;Active&quot;** → a **check-in point was automatically diarised for 2nd June (next week)** → **Anna** was automatically assigned a task to enrol Ramesh onto the nurture email journey. Bob acknowledged that the firm is unlikely to manage the self-build element directly, but noted the system cannot account for this automatically; Paul could manage the nurture communications manually if required.</p><p>### Client: Robert Thurston (DIP Stage)</p><p>Robert Thurston was identified as sitting overdue at the DIP stage. Paul confirmed the DIP had **passed**, with Halifax capping lending at **85% LTV** — acceptable given Thurston is a home mover likely to have more than a 15% deposit. However, Paul had not yet followed up with the client (no response received) and had **not yet issued the Debt Passport**.</p><p>Bob walked Paul through resolving the DIP task as passed, then progressed the workflow to **&quot;Actively Looking&quot;** given the client&#x27;s current status. A **follow-up task was diarised for next week (2nd June)**. Anna was again automatically assigned to place Thurston on the nurture journey for property-buying communications. The Debt Passport was identified as an outstanding action for Paul to address later the same day.</p><p>### Client: Fergus Kane</p><p>Bob flagged that Fergus Kane&#x27;s workflow contained a task relating to **health questions for a protection callback**, with a further prompt asking whether an application had been submitted. Bob directed Paul to address this task, though the detailed resolution was left for Paul to work through independently following the coaching session.</p><p>### General Workflow Discipline</p><p>Across all cases, Bob reinforced a consistent theme: **overdue tasks are a risk to pipeline and performance**. Paul should be updating workflow stages in real time, **delegating administrative and document-chasing tasks to Sean or the support team**, and reserving his own time for advice delivery, application submission, suitability letters, and client relationship management. The instruction was explicit — **do not hold tasks that can be assigned away**.</p><p>---</p><p>## Performance Summary and Strategic Direction</p><p>Bob concluded the meeting with an honest appraisal of Paul&#x27;s current position and a clear strategic directive for the weeks ahead.</p><p>### Appointments and Lead Volume</p><p>Paul has had **44 appointments booked for the current month** against a team target of 40 — a strong lead volume that Bob described as genuinely impressive. However, the conversion of those conversations into completed cases has been limited. Paul&#x27;s own assessment — **&quot;a lot of positive conversations, but not much happening yet&quot;** and **&quot;no quick wins&quot;** — was acknowledged as an honest reflection. The pipeline is building, but not yet converting at the pace required.</p><p>### Mortgage Pipeline</p><p>On mortgages, the outlook is cautiously optimistic. Paul is **broadly on track** to hit his purchase case target, with two potential cases pending documentation. Any cases that do not complete this month are expected to fall into June, which is manageable. The forward pipeline question — **what gives us the next six mortgages and three protection cases?** — was posed as the lens through which Paul should be planning his remortgage and renewal workflow activity.</p><p>### Protection — The Critical Gap</p><p>Protection remains the most significant performance concern, both for Paul specifically and across the wider team. With **one protection application against a quarterly target of eight**, and a **21-day callback report showing a large volume of outstanding opportunities**, the urgency to act is clear. Bob was direct: the longer these cases are left uncontacted, the harder they become to book in and convert — and with the month at the 27th, Paul will not reach the protection written target of 17 this month. The aim must be to carry those opportunities forward productively into June rather than simply losing them.</p><p>### Behavioural and Operational Directives</p><p>Bob closed with four clear operational expectations for Paul going forward:</p><p>- **Maintain consistent client contact** — weekly or fortnightly touchpoints via phone, text, or email, with protected time in the diary to execute these. The specific framing suggested was proactive &quot;how&#x27;s the house buying going?&quot; conversations.</p><p>- **Presence in the office** — Paul should aim to be in for at least **Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday each week** (two and a half days minimum). Where a day must be swapped, an alternative day should be substituted.</p><p>- **Streamline workflow usage** — use the system to delegate effectively, avoiding the trap of holding tasks that support staff can progress.</p><p>- **Focus on protection conversion** — actively work through the 21-day report and MAB callback list to convert outstanding protection opportunities before they go cold.</p><p>Bob undertook to send Paul a follow-up summary email outlining the agreed focus areas.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Send Paul an email with contact details for Harry at Buildstore (self-build specialist referral for Ramesh) — *Completed during meeting*</p><p>- [ ] Send Paul a written summary email outlining agreed focus areas and actions from this meeting — [TBD]</p><p>---</p><p>**@Paul Banyard**</p><p>**Reporting &amp; Administration**</p><p>- [ ] Update the 21-day report with current data — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update the MAB protection callbacks report (was not loading last week) — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Run the Midas Pro &quot;Added to System&quot; report filtered by **product** and then by **adviser** to produce a correct breakdown of purchase vs. renewal mortgage cases and protection apps for the month — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update all MAB action calls in the system — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: New Build Reservation (Thurston / New Build)**</p><p>- [ ] Send the **Commentus** (client communication / suitability note) for the new build reservation client — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Leave the appointment booking with Sean pending document review; proceed to book advice appointment once documents are confirmed as received and verified — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: Ramesh (Self-Build / Plot of Land)**</p><p>- [ ] Contact Ramesh with the Buildstore / Harry referral details and provide an indicative fee figure of approximately £1,000 for when the mainstream application stage is reached — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Diarise a follow-up for Ramesh approximately **four weeks** from now — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: Robert Thurston (DIP Passed)**</p><p>- [ ] Issue the **Debt Passport** to Robert Thurston — [TBD, same day per Bob&#x27;s guidance]</p><p>- [ ] Follow up with Robert Thurston on **2nd June** (check-in call) — 2nd June</p><p>**Client: Tatiana (Protection Review)**</p><p>- [ ] Follow up with Tatiana (and her mother) via email / phone to schedule a protection review — the purchase has now completed — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: Fergus Kane (Protection Callback)**</p><p>- [ ] Address the outstanding health questions task for Fergus Kane and confirm whether a protection application has been submitted — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: Tomlinson**</p><p>- [ ] Ensure the insurance task for Tomlinson is correctly assigned — [TBD]</p><p>**Client: Emily Irwin**</p><p>- [ ] Reassign Emily Irwin&#x27;s task from Roland to Paul Banyard — [TBD]</p><p>**Pipeline &amp; Protection Focus**</p><p>- [ ] Work through the 21-day report and MAB callback list to identify and contact all outstanding protection review opportunities — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure all Platform and Midas workflow tasks are kept up to date in real time; escalate to Bob immediately where workflow options are ambiguous rather than allowing tasks to go overdue — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Actively manage the purchases and remortgage pipeline to progress cases to application stage, including chasing outstanding documents — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Complete and submit outstanding **suitability letters** for applicable cases — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Implement a recurring, diarised schedule for proactive client contact (calls, texts, emails) with existing pipeline clients — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Aim to be present in the office on at least **Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday** each week; substitute an alternative day if one cannot be attended — ongoing</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p># Weekly Performance Review Recap — Paul Banyard</p><p>**Review date:** 27 May 2026  </p><p>**Content creation date:** 27 May 2026 11:46:23  </p><p>**Reviewer:** Bob Duncan  </p><p>**Adviser:** Paul Banyard  </p><p>---</p><p>## 1. Follow-Up From Previous Meeting</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Actions from the previous review were discussed through the outstanding reporting, tracker and CRM discipline issues that remained unresolved at the start of this review.</p><p>### Actions Previously Agreed / Outstanding</p><p>| Previous Action / Area | Status | Owner | Issue |</p><p>|---|---:|---|---|</p><p>| Update the 21-day spreadsheet | Outstanding / not fully updated before review | Paul | Spreadsheet was not fully prepared before the meeting. |</p><p>| Update the protection callback tracker | Outstanding / not fully updated before review | Paul | Paul confirmed the protection calls file had not loaded correctly in the previous week, but it was still not fully updated at the start of this review. |</p><p>| Update written and banked business return | Outstanding / required correction during review | Paul | Reporting needed to be corrected in the meeting, including separating purchase, renewal and protection business. |</p><p>| Clear Platform workflow tasks | Outstanding | Paul | Platform showed **5 late tasks**, dated **26 May 2026**. |</p><p>| Keep workflows aligned to real client position | Partially outstanding | Paul | The new-build reserved client workflow had gone down the wrong path and did not reflect the actual case stage. |</p><p>### Completed vs Outstanding</p><p>**Completed / progressed during the review**</p><p>- Some business figures were clarified during the meeting.</p><p>- Mortgage written business was identified as **£2,792.26**.</p><p>- Protection written business was identified as approximately **£1,100**.</p><p>- Banked business was confirmed as **£4,140.81**.</p><p>- The month-to-date mortgage mix was clarified as:</p><p>  - **4 renewal cases**</p><p>  - **3 purchase cases**</p><p>  - **1 protection application**</p><p>- Some protection callbacks were identified and booked:</p><p>  - **Burgess** — callback booked for **28 May 2026 at 12:30**</p><p>  - **Gilroy** — callback booked for **29 May 2026**</p><p>- Robert Thurston’s DIP position was updated during the review.</p><p>- The new-build / Megan’s friend workflow was moved into a more appropriate position.</p><p>**Outstanding**</p><p>- 21-day spreadsheet still requires full update.</p><p>- Protection callback tracker still requires full update and correction.</p><p>- Written and banked business reporting must be kept updated by product type.</p><p>- **5 overdue Platform tasks** dated **26 May 2026** need clearing.</p><p>- Several case ownership and status issues remain unresolved.</p><p>- Some protection reviews remain unbooked or not fully followed up.</p><p>- Paul still needs to improve CRM workflow accuracy and avoid leaving tasks sitting where the wording is unclear.</p><p>### Reasons for Delay</p><p>- The protection calls file had not loaded correctly in the previous week.</p><p>- CRM workflow wording was being taken too literally, which caused hesitation in progressing some cases.</p><p>- Some tasks were left sitting because Paul was unsure which workflow route to use.</p><p>- Reporting was reactive rather than prepared in advance.</p><p>- There was over-reliance on memory instead of CRM accuracy.</p><p>- Some responsibilities between adviser, admin, case manager and manager were not clear until the review.</p><p>### Repeated Missed Actions / Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- Reporting and tracker preparation are not yet reliable enough.</p><p>- Protection tracking has repeatedly not been updated before review.</p><p>- Workflow tasks are being allowed to become overdue.</p><p>- Where workflow options do not fit perfectly, tasks are at risk of being left unresolved rather than escalated or corrected.</p><p>- Mortgage activity is being prioritised ahead of protection.</p><p>- Paul sometimes holds tasks rather than delegating them to the correct owner.</p><p>- Paul needs to ask Bob for help before CRM tasks fall overdue.</p><p>### Risk Created by Inaction</p><p>- Management visibility is reduced because written and banked figures were not already cleanly broken down.</p><p>- Protection follow-up activity was not clearly evidenced before the meeting.</p><p>- Protection opportunities may be delayed or missed if the callback tracker is not current.</p><p>- Overdue workflow tasks can lead to missed follow-ups and poor client experience.</p><p>- Incorrect workflow stages affect management reporting and case ownership.</p><p>- Cases may drift if documents are requested but workflows are not updated.</p><p>- CRM accuracy risk increases when the system does not reflect the real client position.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul was able to identify renewal and purchase cases once the correct reporting approach was reviewed.</p><p>- Some protection reviews are now in motion.</p><p>- Case-by-case corrections were made during the review.</p><p>- Paul engaged with CRM workflow issues during the meeting.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Key trackers were not ready before the review.</p><p>- Business return reporting had to be rebuilt during the meeting.</p><p>- Protection activity is not being mirrored alongside mortgage activity.</p><p>- Tasks are being left unresolved where workflow wording is unclear.</p><p>- Workflow status does not always match the real client position.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Fully update the 21-day spreadsheet.</p><p>- Fully update the protection callback tracker.</p><p>- Correct inaccurate protection callback entries, including Burgess being entered under Smith.</p><p>- Clear all overdue Platform tasks.</p><p>- Update written and banked business by product type before the next review.</p><p>- Ask Bob for workflow support before tasks become overdue.</p><p>- Keep CRM stages aligned with actual case progress.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — tracker updates, CRM accuracy, overdue tasks, business reporting and adviser-owned follow-up.</p><p>- **Admin / Case Manager:** Sean, Anna and Paula where tasks are assigned.</p><p>- **Other / Manager:** Bob Duncan where guidance or referral details are required.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with risk of decline.**  </p><p>The same reporting, tracker and update discipline issues remain present. Data exists, but it is not being prepared or maintained in advance, creating avoidable review time and weakening performance control.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. Leads Performance Review With CRM Discipline</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul currently has strong lead and appointment volume. Bob confirmed that Paul has had **44 appointments booked in May 2026**, against an expected level of **40**.</p><p>The lead supply position is positive, but conversion is not matching the volume of conversations. Paul described the pipeline as having **“no quick wins”**, with several positive conversations but limited cases progressing quickly.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>No full prior-period lead conversion comparison was provided. However, the current appointment position is ahead of expectation:</p><p>| Metric | Target / Expected | Actual | Position |</p><p>|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Appointments booked in May 2026 | 40 | 44 | Ahead by 4 |</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Lead volume is strong.</p><p>- Appointment numbers are ahead of expectation.</p><p>- Paul is having a high number of client conversations.</p><p>- There are positive conversations taking place with future pipeline potential.</p><p>- The team has supplied a good number of opportunities.</p><p>- Paul has active clients who may progress into mortgage and protection business.</p><p>- Cases reviewed during the meeting were moved into clearer workflow positions.</p><p>- Robert Thurston’s DIP was confirmed as passed and updated to actively looking.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Conversations are not consistently converting into immediate written business.</p><p>- Active leads need clearer next steps.</p><p>- Cases are not consistently being moved through the CRM workflow at the correct stage.</p><p>- Paul has been taking some workflow wording too literally, causing hesitation.</p><p>- Some tasks have been left sitting rather than being resolved, reassigned or diarised.</p><p>- There is a need for better structured ongoing engagement with active leads.</p><p>- Positive conversations may drift without diarised follow-up.</p><p>- CRM tasks may fall overdue where Paul is unsure of the next action.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Strong lead volume may not convert if active clients are not managed consistently.</p><p>- If Paul does not maintain regular contact, future pipeline may weaken.</p><p>- Positive conversations may drift without diary-led follow-up.</p><p>- Clients may disengage if contact frequency is not maintained.</p><p>- Poor CRM discipline could create reporting inaccuracies.</p><p>- Tasks left unresolved could cause missed follow-ups.</p><p>- Future income is at risk if current prospects are not properly nurtured.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain structured contact with active prospects.</p><p>- Use workflow tools properly to move cases on rather than holding them unnecessarily.</p><p>- Use email, text, short calls and CRM tasks to maintain client engagement.</p><p>- Diarise regular client contact at an appropriate rhythm, whether weekly, fortnightly or otherwise suitable.</p><p>- Ensure every active lead has a clear next step.</p><p>- Use CRM workflow and nurture journeys properly.</p><p>- Ask Bob for guidance before allowing tasks to become overdue.</p><p>- Be present in the office on core days where possible to support lead handling and visibility.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with risk of decline.**  </p><p>Lead volume and appointment numbers are strong, but conversion is not following at the same pace. The main issue is not lead availability but workflow discipline, follow-up structure and keeping active prospects warm.</p><p>---</p><p>## 3. “Where Are My Leads?”</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Bob was clear that the issue is not a lack of leads. Paul has had **44 appointments booked in May 2026**, exceeding the expected level of **40**.</p><p>The key issue is whether Paul is managing those leads effectively enough to turn them into future mortgage and protection business.</p><p>### DIP Volume Trend</p><p>Available DIP-related case data discussed:</p><p>| Client | DIP Position | Notes |</p><p>|---|---|---|</p><p>| Megan’s friend / new-build reservation case | DIP completed | ID check completed; documents requested; case moving towards recommendation once documents reviewed. |</p><p>| Robert Thurston | DIP passed | Halifax DIP passed at **85% LTV**; client now marked as actively looking. |</p><p>| New-build reserved client | DIP / debt requirement completed | Client has reserved a new-build unit; documents requested; workflow needed correction. |</p><p>No full DIP volume trend against previous weeks was provided.</p><p>### Lead Quality vs Adviser Behaviour</p><p>- Lead quality and volume appear sufficient.</p><p>- Adviser behaviour is the key performance variable.</p><p>- Paul has positive conversations but must improve how he follows up, diarises and progresses cases.</p><p>- Some cases are stalling because CRM workflow actions are not being completed promptly.</p><p>### Engagement Frequency</p><p>- Paul needs to maintain regular contact with active clients.</p><p>- Contact rhythm should be diarised using calls, texts, emails and CRM tasks.</p><p>- Active prospects should not be left without a clear next contact date.</p><p>### Stalled Cases / Reporting Inconsistencies</p><p>- The new-build client workflow had gone down the wrong route.</p><p>- Robert Thurston’s DIP outcome had not been updated promptly before the review.</p><p>- Ramesh had been left sitting while Paul waited for referral details from Bob.</p><p>- Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane had unresolved protection / application status items.</p><p>- Some protection callback tracker entries were inaccurate, including Burgess being entered under Smith.</p><p>- Emily Irwin was incorrectly showing under Roland and should be Paul’s case.</p><p>- Charlotte Morrison was not recognised during the review and needs ownership/status confirmation.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Appointment numbers are strong.</p><p>- There are enough leads to support the required performance.</p><p>- Paul is having positive conversations.</p><p>- Some CRM issues were corrected during the meeting.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Pipeline has limited immediate conversion.</p><p>- CRM workflows need to be used more consistently.</p><p>- Active leads need clearer next steps.</p><p>- Some cases sit unresolved due to uncertainty over workflow wording.</p><p>- Reporting and ownership clarity are not yet consistent.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Active leads may become stale if not contacted regularly.</p><p>- CRM inaccuracies may distort pipeline and performance reporting.</p><p>- Future income may be lost if prospects are not nurtured.</p><p>- Missed or delayed follow-ups may weaken client engagement.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain contact with active clients at an agreed rhythm.</p><p>- Ensure every active lead has a clear next step.</p><p>- Use CRM workflow and nurture journeys properly.</p><p>- Correct stale or inaccurate statuses.</p><p>- Ask Bob for help when the correct CRM route is unclear.</p><p>- Maintain office presence on core days where possible.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>- **Other / Manager:** Bob Duncan for workflow support where required.</p><p>- **Admin:** Anna where nurture journey tasks are assigned.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>Lead supply is strong, but conversion and structured follow-up need improvement to prevent the pipeline from becoming passive.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. Applications / Cases in Progress</p><p>### Client: Megan’s Friend / New-Build Reservation Case</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Megan’s friend / new-build reservation case |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | DIP completed; ID check completed; documents requested; moving towards recommendation booking once documents are reviewed |</p><p>| Owner | Paul / Sean |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Paul had completed the ID check and DIP. Bob confirmed duplicate or unnecessary tasks could be cleared because the DIP had already been completed.</p><p>The case is now sitting with documents requested. Bob explained that the next step is for Sean to review the documents and confirm whether anything is missing, while Paul completes his adviser comments. Once documents are complete and verified, the case should be booked for recommendation.</p><p>Sean now has a task to **update adviser on documents**.</p><p>#### Delays / Missing Documents / SLA Issues</p><p>- Documents need to be reviewed.</p><p>- Recommendation appointment is not yet booked.</p><p>- Paul’s comments still need completing.</p><p>- The case had risked sitting incorrectly because Paul was waiting for workflow wording that exactly matched the situation.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- ID check passed.</p><p>- DIP completed.</p><p>- Documents have been requested.</p><p>- Case has been moved into a more appropriate workflow position.</p><p>- Sean has ownership of the document review task.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Paul had not fully understood which workflow route to use after DIP.</p><p>- The case risked sitting incorrectly.</p><p>- Recommendation appointment was not yet booked because documents still needed checking.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Recommendation appointment may be delayed if documents are not checked promptly.</p><p>- Case may not be ready to move forward if Paul does not complete comments.</p><p>- Incorrect workflow stages can cause overdue tasks or wrong ownership.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Paul to complete adviser comments.</p><p>- Sean to review documents and confirm missing items.</p><p>- Once documents are complete and verified, Paul to book recommendation.</p><p>- Paul to ask Bob for guidance if unsure of the workflow.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — comments and case progression.</p><p>- **Case Manager / Admin:** Sean — document review and adviser update.</p><p>- **Other / Manager:** Bob Duncan — workflow guidance if needed.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving.**  </p><p>The case was at risk of sitting incorrectly, but the workflow was corrected during the review and ownership was clarified.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: New-Build Reserved Client</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | New-build reserved client |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Client has reserved a new-build unit; documents requested; application stage pending documents |</p><p>| Owner | Paul / Paula |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>The workflow had gone down the wrong path. The client has reserved a new-build unit, documents have been requested, and Paula is being set up to contact them.</p><p>The workflow needed correcting to reflect:</p><p>- DIP / debt requirement completed</p><p>- Client has reserved a new-build unit</p><p>- Documents required</p><p>- Application stage pending documents</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Client has reserved a new-build unit.</p><p>- Documents have been requested.</p><p>- Paula is being set up to contact the client.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Workflow did not accurately reflect the real case position.</p><p>- The case was sitting in the wrong workflow route.</p><p>- There was uncertainty over whether the next step should show active looking, appointment booked, documents requested or application stage.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Case may drift if workflow is not corrected.</p><p>- Client contact may be missed or duplicated if ownership is unclear.</p><p>- Management reporting may be inaccurate.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Correct workflow route.</p><p>- Mark documents required.</p><p>- Ensure document request communication has been sent where not already sent.</p><p>- Confirm Paula’s client contact task.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — workflow correction and adviser follow-up.</p><p>- **Admin:** Paula — client contact where assigned.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving.**  </p><p>The issue was identified and the required workflow state was clarified, but correction still needs to be completed.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Ramesh</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Ramesh |</p><p>| Lender | Not applicable / external referral likely |</p><p>| Current stage | Unable to directly assist with self-build / land purchase; referral route to Buildstore / Harry Connor identified |</p><p>| Owner | Bob / Paul / Anna |</p><p>| Deadline | Follow-up task for **2 June 2026** |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Ramesh is looking at buying a plot of land and may require self-build support. Paul had updated some information but had left the case sitting because he was waiting for Bob to provide referral details.</p><p>Bob confirmed he would email Paul the details for **Harry Connor at Buildstore**.</p><p>The case was moved into an active / future contact position, with a check-in task created for **2 June 2026**. A task was also created for Anna to place the client on a nurture journey, although Paul queried whether this was appropriate because the client is likely buying a self-build property that the firm may not manage directly.</p><p>#### Delays / Missing Documents / SLA Issues</p><p>- Case had stalled while waiting for referral details.</p><p>- Fee structure information had not been issued because the case had not progressed to full advice.</p><p>- Suitability of the standard nurture journey is unclear.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul recognised the case may be outside standard lending support.</p><p>- Bob identified a referral route via Harry Connor / Buildstore.</p><p>- Future check-in task has been created.</p><p>- Client has not been closed off and remains in future contact.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Task had been left sitting while Paul waited for information from Bob.</p><p>- Fee expectations have not yet been clarified.</p><p>- Nurture communications may not align with the self-build nature of the enquiry.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Client may disengage if the referral is not provided promptly.</p><p>- Inappropriate nurture communication may confuse the client.</p><p>- Ownership could remain unclear between Paul, Bob, Anna and the external referral contact.</p><p>- Fee expectations may be unclear.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Bob to send Paul referral details for Harry Connor / Buildstore.</p><p>- Paul to provide Ramesh with referral information.</p><p>- Paul to include indicative fee guidance, explaining the fee could be roughly **£1,000**, subject to confirmation.</p><p>- Paul to diarise and complete the **2 June 2026** follow-up.</p><p>- Paul / Anna to review whether the standard nurture journey is appropriate.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Other / Manager:** Bob Duncan — send referral details.</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — contact Ramesh, provide referral, clarify indicative fees and manage CRM follow-up.</p><p>- **Admin:** Anna — nurture journey task, subject to suitability.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving.**  </p><p>The case had stalled due to missing referral details, but a path forward and follow-up date were created during the review.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Protection callback / health questions outstanding; application submission/status needs checking |</p><p>| Owner | Paul |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Bob flagged an outstanding task for Paul to review. The system is asking about health questions and whether an application has been submitted.</p><p>In the wider task review, Fergus Kane was also identified as having an overdue workflow item where application submission/status needed checking. These appear to refer to the same or closely related outstanding case item and require clarification in CRM.</p><p>#### Delays / Missing Documents / SLA Issues</p><p>- Case was not worked through during the meeting.</p><p>- No confirmed update on whether the protection application has been submitted.</p><p>- No confirmed outcome on health questions.</p><p>- Workflow item is overdue or unresolved.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- CRM has surfaced the outstanding protection-related task.</p><p>- The case remains visible to Paul.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Task was not progressed during the review.</p><p>- Application status remains unclear.</p><p>- Health questions remain unresolved.</p><p>- CRM may be inaccurate.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Protection business may be delayed or lost.</p><p>- Client may disengage if follow-up is slow.</p><p>- CRM reporting may remain inaccurate.</p><p>- Application may be delayed or incorrectly reported.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Paul to review the task.</p><p>- Paul to confirm whether health questions remain outstanding.</p><p>- Paul to confirm whether the protection application has been submitted.</p><p>- Paul to check application/status position.</p><p>- CRM to be updated with outcome and next step.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>The task exists but was not progressed during the review, creating risk around protection conversion and CRM accuracy.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Robert Thurston</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Robert Thurston |</p><p>| Lender | Halifax |</p><p>| Current stage | DIP passed; actively looking; passport not yet completed |</p><p>| Owner | Paul / Anna |</p><p>| Deadline | Follow-up callback on **2 June 2026** |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Paul confirmed that the DIP passed. Halifax limited the case to **85% LTV**, which Paul believes should be acceptable because Robert is a home mover and is likely to have at least a **15% deposit**.</p><p>The client had not responded following contact, and the DIP outcome had not been properly moved through the workflow before the review.</p><p>During the review, Bob and Paul updated the task to show that the DIP passed. Robert was marked as **actively looking**, and the system created a callback for **2 June 2026**. Anna will also place him onto a nurture journey for buying property in Scotland.</p><p>The passport has not yet been completed.</p><p>#### Delays / Missing Documents / SLA Issues</p><p>- DIP outcome had not been updated promptly.</p><p>- Passport not yet completed.</p><p>- Client has not responded.</p><p>- Case was sitting at DIP stage without the correct outcome being resolved.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- DIP passed.</p><p>- Halifax outcome is acceptable based on expected deposit.</p><p>- Client is now recorded as actively looking.</p><p>- Follow-up has been diarised for **2 June 2026**.</p><p>- Nurture journey will support ongoing engagement.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- DIP outcome had not been updated promptly in CRM.</p><p>- Passport remains outstanding.</p><p>- Paul has not had a response from the client.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Client may drift if Paul does not follow up.</p><p>- Passport delay may slow future progress.</p><p>- CRM reporting may be inaccurate if DIP outcomes are not updated immediately.</p><p>- Client may disengage or proceed elsewhere.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Paul to complete the passport.</p><p>- Paul to follow up with Robert Thurston.</p><p>- Paul to keep the **2 June 2026** callback.</p><p>- Paul to update DIP outcomes in CRM as soon as known.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — passport, follow-up and CRM update.</p><p>- **Admin:** Anna — nurture journey.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving.**  </p><p>The case was previously sitting incorrectly, but it has now been moved forward with a clear DIP outcome, active buyer status and diarised follow-up.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Rachel</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Rachel |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Active-looking workflow item overdue |</p><p>| Owner | Paul |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Rachel was identified as having an overdue active-looking workflow item. Status requires review and next step update.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Case has been identified as requiring attention.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Workflow item is overdue.</p><p>- Current client status and next step are unclear.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Lead may stall.</p><p>- Client may disengage without a clear follow-up plan.</p><p>- CRM reporting may be inaccurate.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Review active-looking status.</p><p>- Update CRM with next step.</p><p>- Diarise follow-up as appropriate.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>The case is visible but overdue and needs action to prevent drift.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Stephen Reed / Stevenson</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Stephen Reed / Stevenson |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Appeared on 21-day / protection review list; ownership/status needs checking |</p><p>| Owner | Paul |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>This case appeared on the 21-day / protection review list. Ownership and status needed checking.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Ownership/status was unclear during the review.</p><p>- Protection review position was not evidenced clearly.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Case may be missed if ownership is not corrected.</p><p>- Protection opportunity may be delayed.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm ownership/status.</p><p>- Update CRM and tracker.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>The case requires clarification before performance or protection opportunity can be managed effectively.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Emily Irwin</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Emily Irwin |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Incorrect ownership showing |</p><p>| Owner | Paul |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Emily Irwin was incorrectly showing under Roland. It was confirmed this should be Paul’s case and needs correcting.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Ownership and reporting remain inaccurate.</p><p>- Follow-up may be missed if assigned to the wrong adviser.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Correct ownership from Roland to Paul.</p><p>- Confirm current status and next step.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>Ownership clarity needs correcting to avoid reporting and follow-up risk.</p><p>---</p><p>### Client: Charlotte Morrison</p><p>| Field | Detail |</p><p>|---|---|</p><p>| Client | Charlotte Morrison |</p><p>| Lender | Not specified |</p><p>| Current stage | Not recognised during review; ownership/status needs confirmation |</p><p>| Owner | Paul |</p><p>| Deadline | Not specified |</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>Charlotte Morrison was not recognised during the review and needs ownership/status confirmation.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Case may drift without clear owner.</p><p>- CRM may be inaccurate.</p><p>- Follow-up may be missed.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm status and ownership.</p><p>- Update CRM with next step.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>The case cannot be controlled until ownership and status are confirmed.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. Protection In Progress</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Protection is the main performance concern.</p><p>The review identified:</p><p>- Quarterly protection target: **8 insurance applications**</p><p>- Current position: **1 insurance application**</p><p>- Shortfall: **7 insurance applications**</p><p>- Mortgage applications: **6**</p><p>- Protection applications: **1**</p><p>- Protection conversion: **16%**</p><p>The 21-day protection callback report shows a large number of opportunities requiring attention. Protection activity is materially behind target and not being driven with the same focus as mortgage business.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>No full prior comparison was provided, but the recurring issue is that protection callback tracking was not fully updated before the review, and protection activity remains below expectation.</p><p>### Protection Cases Discussed</p><p>| Client | Current Position | Issue / Risk | Owner |</p><p>|---|---|---|---|</p><p>| Raymond | Existing IP, life and critical illness cover previously sold. Remortgage case reverted to product transfer after down valuation due to property being above commercial. No known change in circumstances. Protection review still to be followed up. | Review not yet booked; missed opportunity to confirm ongoing suitability. | Paul / Admin |</p><p>| Claire and Callum | Employer benefits details still required before proper protection review. Sean was following up. | Protection review cannot be completed without benefits details. | Sean |</p><p>| Tatiana | Protection review required with Tatiana and her mother after mortgage review and completion. | Client not yet booked; adviser follow-up may not be best use of time and should be assigned for booking where appropriate. | Paul / Admin |</p><p>| Tomlinson | Insurance already completed. | Tracker needs updating to avoid duplicate chase. | Paul |</p><p>| Gilroy | Callback booked for **29 May 2026**. | Must ensure appointment proceeds and outcome is logged. | Paul |</p><p>| Burgess | Callback booked for **28 May 2026 at 12:30**. | Entry was initially placed under Smith, creating CRM accuracy risk. | Paul |</p><p>| Smith | Incorrect entry made under this client during tracker update. | Wrong client record and follow-up risk. | Paul |</p><p>| Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane | Outstanding health questions and possible application submission status. | Protection application may stall or be lost. | Paul |</p><p>| Stephen Reed / Stevenson | Appeared on 21-day / protection list. | Ownership/status unclear. | Paul |</p><p>### Cases Without Protection Discussion / Missed Cross-Sell Opportunities</p><p>- Protection is not being mirrored alongside mortgage activity.</p><p>- Raymond’s ongoing protection suitability review has not yet been booked after remortgage / product transfer activity.</p><p>- Tatiana’s protection review with her mother remains outstanding after mortgage review and completion.</p><p>- Claire and Callum cannot progress until employer benefits details are obtained.</p><p>- Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane has unresolved health question and application status issues.</p><p>- The 21-day callback report contains further opportunities that need to be worked quickly.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- At least **1 protection application** is recorded.</p><p>- Protection opportunities exist within the callback reports.</p><p>- The gap is visible and measurable.</p><p>- Some protection callbacks are now booked.</p><p>- Paul is identifying protection opportunities through workflow review.</p><p>- Existing client protection positions are known in some cases, such as Raymond.</p><p>- This is a known focus area for Paul and the wider team.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Protection conversion is low at **16%**.</p><p>- Paul has only **1 protection application** from **6 mortgage applications**.</p><p>- Protection is not being given the same focus as mortgage activity.</p><p>- Protection callbacks are not being acted on quickly enough.</p><p>- Protection tracking was not fully updated before the meeting.</p><p>- Some protection reviews are still not booked.</p><p>- There is over-reliance on adviser follow-up where admin booking support may be more efficient.</p><p>- Opportunities become harder to convert the longer they are left.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Gap between **1 current insurance application** and the **8-application quarterly target** is significant.</p><p>- Protection business may be missed if callbacks are delayed.</p><p>- Delayed reviews reduce written business and create revenue shortfall.</p><p>- Longer delays make clients harder to re-engage.</p><p>- Paul is unlikely to write the required protection business for May 2026.</p><p>- Future revenue is exposed if protection does not catch up in June 2026.</p><p>- Weak tracking may cause duplicate chases, missed reviews or unclear ownership.</p><p>- CRM inaccuracy may hide the true position if callbacks and application statuses are not updated.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Prioritise the 21-day protection callback report.</p><p>- Review all outstanding protection callbacks.</p><p>- Chase and book outstanding protection reviews.</p><p>- Assign booking tasks to admin where appropriate.</p><p>- Complete booked protection appointments and update CRM outcomes immediately.</p><p>- Ensure protection is chased alongside purchase and remortgage activity.</p><p>- Resolve the Fergus Gain / Fergus Kane protection task.</p><p>- Build protection activity into the June 2026 pipeline plan.</p><p>- Identify the next **3 protection cases** for June 2026.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard — protection follow-up, CRM updates, callback outcomes and pipeline planning.</p><p>- **Admin:** Admin support where booking is required.</p><p>- **Case Manager / Admin:** Sean for Claire and Callum employer benefits follow-up.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Declining.**  </p><p>Protection activity is materially behind target. With only **1 protection application from 6 mortgage applications**, conversion is weak and delayed callbacks are increasing the risk of missed business.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6. Written Business – This Month</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>| Metric | Current Position |</p><p>|---|---:|</p><p>| Mortgage written business | **£2,792.26** |</p><p>| Protection written business | Approximately **£1,100** |</p><p>| Purchase applications | **3** |</p><p>| Renewal applications | **4** |</p><p>| Protection applications | **1** |</p><p>| Banked business | **£4,140.81** |</p><p>| Purchase application target | **5** |</p><p>| Purchase application shortfall | **2** |</p><p>| Required written number for May 2026 | **17** |</p><p>| Expected result | Bob stated Paul is **not going to write the 17 required this month** |</p><p>Bob stated that Paul is broadly **in line to hit the number of mortgages needed**, but this remains **hit or miss by the end of May 2026** and depends on documents being received and progressed.</p><p>Paul confirmed there are potentially **2 further purchase cases**, but one is still dependent on documents being received.</p><p>### Written vs Same Month Last Year</p><p>No same-month-last-year written business comparison was provided.</p><p>### Pipeline Expected to Write</p><p>- Two potential purchase cases may close the immediate purchase shortfall.</p><p>- One of those cases is still dependent on documents.</p><p>- Some mortgage cases expected in May 2026 may slip into June 2026.</p><p>- Mortgage numbers remain potentially achievable, but protection is materially behind.</p><p>### Likelihood of Target Being Hit</p><p>- Purchase case target may still be reached if the two potential cases proceed.</p><p>- Overall written target of **17** is not expected to be achieved.</p><p>- Protection target is significantly behind and unlikely to recover fully before month end.</p><p>### Adviser Confidence Level</p><p>- Paul identified potential purchase cases but acknowledged document dependency.</p><p>- Bob described mortgage delivery as “hit or miss” by month end.</p><p>- Paul described the current pipeline as having “no quick wins”.</p><p>### Reasons for Performance Difference</p><p>- Appointment volume is strong, but conversion is slow.</p><p>- Document delays are holding back some cases.</p><p>- Protection activity is weak.</p><p>- Reporting breakdown was not prepared clearly before the review.</p><p>- Some cases were not in the correct CRM workflow stage.</p><p>- CRM tasks and next steps need tighter ownership.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has identified two potential purchase cases that may close the gap.</p><p>- Paul is aware that one case is waiting on documents.</p><p>- Mortgage numbers are close enough to target to remain achievable.</p><p>- Appointment volume supports future written business.</p><p>- Some cases expected to write in May 2026 may support June 2026 if they slip.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Written business is not converting quickly enough from appointment volume.</p><p>- May 2026 target of **17** is not expected to be achieved.</p><p>- Protection written business is not strong enough.</p><p>- Document delays may affect whether mortgages land in-month.</p><p>- Reporting breakdown was not prepared clearly before the review.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If the two potential purchase cases do not proceed, written business will remain below expectation.</p><p>- Waiting on documents may delay submission and push business into June 2026.</p><p>- Protection shortfall may increase pressure in June and July 2026.</p><p>- Slipped business may not materialise if active prospects are not managed well.</p><p>- Continued underperformance in protection will increase pressure on future months.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Chase documents on the purchase case currently waiting.</p><p>- Push the two potential purchase cases to application where appropriate.</p><p>- Focus on cases where documents are outstanding.</p><p>- Ensure document review tasks are sitting with the correct owner, such as Sean where appropriate.</p><p>- Progress ready cases to recommendation and application.</p><p>- Keep written business tracker updated by product type:</p><p>  - Purchases</p><p>  - Renewals</p><p>  - Protection</p><p>- Build a June 2026 catch-up plan, including additional mortgages and protection cases.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>- **Case Manager / Admin:** Sean where document review is required.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>There is potential business available, but conversion depends on faster document collection, clearer tracking, stronger follow-up and improved protection activity.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7. Banked Business – This Month</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Banked business was reviewed at **£4,140.81**.</p><p>### Banked vs Last Year</p><p>No banked business comparison with the same month last year was provided.</p><p>### Pipeline Still to Complete</p><p>- Written mortgage cases still need to improve to support future banked business.</p><p>- Purchase pipeline includes two potential cases, one dependent on documents.</p><p>- Cases slipping from May 2026 may support June 2026 if managed properly.</p><p>### Slippage Risk</p><p>- If written business does not improve, future banked business will be exposed.</p><p>- Document delays could push written and future banked income into later periods.</p><p>- Weak protection conversion reduces future income potential.</p><p>### Revenue Exposure</p><p>- Banked figure is known, but product-level breakdown needs to be clearer.</p><p>- Without clean reporting, it is harder to forecast slippage or understand which product types are driving income.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Banked figure is known and was confirmed during the meeting.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Banked business report was not prepared in advance in a structured way.</p><p>- Product-level breakdown needs to be clearer.</p><p>- Written and banked reporting discipline needs improvement.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Forecasting is weaker without clean reporting.</p><p>- Future banked business is exposed if written business does not improve.</p><p>- Slippage risk cannot be properly managed without product-level reporting.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Keep banked reporting updated.</p><p>- Break down banked and written figures by product type before the next review.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>The current banked figure is clear, but reporting discipline and product-level breakdown need to improve.</p><p>---</p><p>## 8. Renewal / Remortgage Income</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Renewal and remortgage activity was discussed as part of the business mix and pipeline review.</p><p>Current month-to-date mortgage mix includes:</p><p>- **4 renewal cases**</p><p>- **3 purchase cases**</p><p>- **1 protection application**</p><p>Bob also raised the need to understand expected income from the remortgage workflow.</p><p>### Clients / Cases in Pipeline</p><p>| Client / Area | Current Position | Issue / Risk | Owner |</p><p>|---|---|---|---|</p><p>| Raymond | Remortgage case reverted to product transfer after down valuation due to property being above commercial. | Protection review still needs follow-up. | Paul / Admin |</p><p>| Remortgage workflow | Expected income not clearly quantified. | June 2026 income forecast is uncertain. | Paul |</p><p>| Purchase / renewal reporting | Renewal cases identified during the review. | Product-level reporting was not prepared in advance. | Paul |</p><p>### Reviews Booked</p><p>- Protection callbacks booked for Burgess and Gilroy.</p><p>- No specific remortgage review booking schedule was provided beyond Raymond and general workflow review.</p><p>### Insurance Gaps</p><p>- Raymond requires protection review follow-up despite existing IP, life and critical illness cover.</p><p>- Protection should be reviewed alongside remortgage and product transfer activity.</p><p>- General protection conversion is weak at **16%**.</p><p>### Clients Still to Contact</p><p>- Raymond protection review follow-up.</p><p>- Remortgage workflow opportunities need review.</p><p>- 21-day protection callback opportunities need prioritising.</p><p>### Forecast vs Expectation</p><p>- Remortgage workflow expectations were not clearly quantified.</p><p>- Bob highlighted the need to review remortgage workflow opportunities to support June 2026 income.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Renewal cases were identified during the review.</p><p>- Raymond’s position is understood.</p><p>- Remortgage workflow was identified as an area for income review.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Remortgage income expectations were not quantified.</p><p>- Product-level reporting was not prepared in advance.</p><p>- Protection reviews are not consistently being completed alongside remortgage activity.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Income forecast for June 2026 remains uncertain.</p><p>- Insurance opportunities may be missed on remortgage / product transfer clients.</p><p>- Weak reporting makes it harder to identify income drivers.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Review remortgage workflow opportunities.</p><p>- Quantify expected remortgage income.</p><p>- Follow up Raymond’s protection review.</p><p>- Ensure protection is reviewed alongside remortgage and renewal activity.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>- **Admin:** Admin support for booking where appropriate.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>Renewal and remortgage activity exists, but income expectations and associated protection opportunities need clearer ownership and reporting.</p><p>---</p><p>## 9. Remortgage Opportunities</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Remortgage opportunities were discussed primarily through Raymond and the wider remortgage workflow.</p><p>### Clients Chased / Disengaged / Re-Engagement Strategy</p><p>| Client / Area | Position | Required Re-Engagement |</p><p>|---|---|---|</p><p>| Raymond | Remortgage reverted to product transfer after down valuation due to property being above commercial. Existing protection in place. | Follow up protection review to confirm suitability and identify any changes. |</p><p>| Remortgage workflow | Expected income not clearly quantified. | Review CRM workflow and identify opportunities for June 2026. |</p><p>### Weak Follow-Up Trends</p><p>- Protection is not being consistently chased alongside remortgage / mortgage activity.</p><p>- Remortgage workflow expectations are not clearly reported.</p><p>- Callback and review tracker discipline is weak.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Raymond’s revised route to product transfer is understood.</p><p>- Remortgage workflow has been identified as an area to review.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Protection follow-up after remortgage / product transfer activity remains incomplete.</p><p>- Expected remortgage income has not been quantified.</p><p>- CRM and tracker records are not consistently updated.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Remortgage income may be under-forecast or missed.</p><p>- Protection cross-sell opportunities may be delayed or lost.</p><p>- Clients may drift if re-engagement is not diarised.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Review remortgage workflow opportunities.</p><p>- Follow up Raymond protection review.</p><p>- Ensure all remortgage-related next steps are logged in CRM.</p><p>- Use admin booking support where appropriate.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>- **Admin:** Admin support where booking is needed.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>Opportunities exist, but they are not yet being actively quantified or consistently linked to protection review activity.</p><p>---</p><p>## 10. Projected Income</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Bob highlighted the need for Paul to think ahead about the pipeline required for June 2026.</p><p>The focus should be on creating the next:</p><p>- **6 mortgage cases**</p><p>- **3 protection cases**</p><p>Bob also raised the need to understand expected income from the remortgage workflow.</p><p>### Pipeline vs Budget</p><p>- Appointment volume is strong, with **44 appointments** against an expectation of **40**.</p><p>- May 2026 written target of **17** is not expected to be achieved.</p><p>- Purchase target is **5**, with **3** currently achieved and **2** potential cases identified.</p><p>- Protection target is **8 insurance applications** for the quarter, with only **1** currently achieved.</p><p>- June 2026 requires a clear pipeline plan for **6 mortgage cases** and **3 protection cases**.</p><p>### Shortfall</p><p>- Purchase cases are **2 short** of target.</p><p>- Protection applications are **7 short** of the quarterly target.</p><p>- May 2026 written business is expected to fall short of the required **17**.</p><p>- Protection conversion is only **16%**.</p><p>### Plan to Close Gap</p><p>- Progress the two potential purchase cases to application where appropriate.</p><p>- Chase outstanding purchase documents immediately.</p><p>- Prioritise the 21-day protection callback report.</p><p>- Identify the next **6 mortgage cases** for June 2026.</p><p>- Identify the next **3 protection cases** for June 2026.</p><p>- Review remortgage workflow opportunities.</p><p>- Maintain regular contact with active prospects.</p><p>- Use CRM workflows to delegate, diarise and move cases forward.</p><p>### Behaviour Impacting Results</p><p>- Cases are drifting where CRM workflow wording is unclear.</p><p>- Paul sometimes leaves tasks sitting rather than escalating or asking for help.</p><p>- Protection is not being given equal priority with mortgage activity.</p><p>- Reporting is reactive rather than prepared in advance.</p><p>- Active clients need stronger diary-led engagement.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has active opportunities and strong appointment volume.</p><p>- Some May 2026 cases may slip into June 2026 and support next month’s starting pipeline.</p><p>- Protection callbacks exist and could still convert if worked quickly.</p><p>- Some mortgage numbers remain close enough to target to be achievable.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Future pipeline confidence is not yet strong enough.</p><p>- Protection activity is not keeping pace.</p><p>- Remortgage workflow expectations were not clearly quantified.</p><p>- Current behaviour risks leaving too much income dependent on cases drifting from one month to the next.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Revenue shortfall if slipped mortgage cases do not convert.</p><p>- Protection income risk due to delayed callbacks.</p><p>- Pipeline uncertainty for June 2026.</p><p>- Failure to manage active prospects could affect mortgage and protection income.</p><p>- Poor CRM updates and overdue tasks increase the risk of cases drifting and income being delayed.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Identify pipeline for the next **6 mortgage cases**.</p><p>- Identify and prioritise the next **3 protection cases**.</p><p>- Review remortgage workflow opportunities.</p><p>- Build a June 2026 catch-up plan.</p><p>- Use CRM workflows to delegate, diarise and move cases forward.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant.**  </p><p>There is opportunity in the pipeline, but income remains uncertain until Paul tightens follow-up, protection activity, reporting and workflow management.</p><p>---</p><p>## 11. Overall Performance Diagnosis</p><p>### Adviser Strengths This Week</p><p>- Strong appointment volume, with **44 appointments booked in May 2026** against an expectation of **40**.</p><p>- Positive client conversations.</p><p>- Mortgage case awareness is stronger than protection case control.</p><p>- Paul identified two potential purchase cases that may close the monthly purchase gap.</p><p>- Paul understands where some key protection cases sit once reviewed.</p><p>- Willingness to work through CRM workflow issues during the review.</p><p>- Mortgage numbers are still potentially in line with required levels.</p><p>- Paul identified case complexity, such as the self-build / land purchase enquiry for Ramesh.</p><p>- Some case-by-case improvements were made during the review.</p><p>### Main Risks</p><p>- Protection activity is well below target.</p><p>- Protection conversion is low at **16%**.</p><p>- Only **1 protection application** from **6 mortgage applications**.</p><p>- Protection is **7 applications short** of the quarterly target of **8**.</p><p>- May 2026 written business target of **17** is unlikely to be achieved.</p><p>- Purchase activity is **2 cases short** of target.</p><p>- CRM workflow discipline is inconsistent.</p><p>- Tracker and reporting preparation are not reliable enough.</p><p>- Overdue tasks create follow-up and ownership risk.</p><p>- Some case statuses and ownership positions were unclear.</p><p>- Active leads may drift without structured contact.</p><p>- Some workflow ownership gaps exist between adviser, admin and case manager until tasks are correctly assigned.</p><p>### Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- Mortgage activity is being prioritised ahead of protection.</p><p>- Reporting and tracking appear reactive rather than prepared in advance.</p><p>- There is reliance on memory instead of CRM accuracy.</p><p>- Paul sometimes takes workflow wording too literally rather than applying the logical next stage.</p><p>- Tasks can be left sitting where clarity is missing.</p><p>- Follow-up needs to become more structured and diary-led.</p><p>- Paul needs to use the system to delegate tasks to the correct owner rather than holding too much himself.</p><p>- There was hesitation around CRM workflow decisions.</p><p>- Where workflow options do not fit perfectly, tasks are at risk of being left unresolved rather than escalated or corrected.</p><p>### Accountability Concerns</p><p>- Paul must update DIP outcomes promptly.</p><p>- Paul must not allow tasks to become late because of uncertainty.</p><p>- Paul must complete outstanding passports and comments.</p><p>- Protection callbacks need faster action.</p><p>- CRM must show clear ownership and next steps.</p><p>- Paul must update business reporting before review rather than correcting during the meeting.</p><p>- Paul must ensure protection callback records are accurate and not entered under the wrong client.</p><p>### Where Management Support Is Required</p><p>- Bob to support Paul with workflow interpretation while Paul builds familiarity.</p><p>- Bob to provide referral details for Harry Connor / Buildstore.</p><p>- Paul should proactively ask Bob for help where CRM workflow is unclear.</p><p>- Continued support on using Midas / Platform reports correctly, especially reporting by product and adviser.</p><p>- Clarification on workflow routes where client journeys do not match obvious CRM options.</p><p>- Sean to support document review where tasks are assigned to him.</p><p>- Admin support should be used for booking protection reviews where adviser time is better spent progressing advice and applications.</p><p>### Focus for Next Week</p><p>- Clear all overdue workflow tasks.</p><p>- Keep CRM stages aligned to the real case position.</p><p>- Eliminate incorrect or misplaced tracker entries.</p><p>- Progress the two potential purchase cases to application where documents and advice allow.</p><p>- Chase outstanding documents immediately.</p><p>- Increase protection focus and book outstanding reviews.</p><p>- Prioritise the 21-day protection callback report.</p><p>- Update written and banked business by product type before the next review.</p><p>- Ensure every protection callback has a clear owner, date and outcome logged.</p><p>- Improve speed and consistency of follow-up with active clients.</p><p>- Eliminate CRM tasks sitting unresolved due to uncertainty.</p><p>- Progress stalled cases to the correct workflow stage.</p><p>- Complete outstanding passports, comments and protection updates.</p><p>- Identify the next **6 mortgage cases** and **3 protection cases** for June 2026.</p><p>- Ask for help before any CRM task falls overdue.</p><p>- Maintain office presence on core days where possible.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with areas improving, but protection declining.**  </p><p>Paul’s appointment volume and mortgage opportunities are positive, and several cases improved during the review. However, CRM discipline, reporting preparation and protection activity are holding back performance. Protection is the clearest declining area because activity is materially behind target and callbacks are not being acted on quickly enough.</p><p>---</p><p>## 12. Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---|---|---|</p><p>| Update the 21-day spreadsheet fully | 21-day tracker | Paul B